{"reference":"James 3","book":"James","chapter":3,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"language_literary","title":"Speech and Its Power","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"James piles up images—a bit guiding a horse, a small rudder turning a ship, a fire spreading through wood, untamable animals, a spring, and fruit trees—to make one point from several angles: speech can have effects far larger than its size, and it can expose a troubling inconsistency between what a person praises and what that person says about others.","verse_refs":["James 3:3-12"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_james_003_0c308fc1eba7063a94a324c2"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Teachers, Wholeness, and Wisdom","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The warning to teachers is immediately connected with failures in speech. In this setting, the “perfect” person of verse 2 is best understood as whole or complete, rather than as a claim that someone has reached sinless perfection. The chapter then tests wisdom by visible conduct: wisdom from above is marked by purity, peace, gentleness, openness to reason, mercy, good fruits, impartiality, and sincerity, in contrast to jealousy and rivalry.","verse_refs":["James 3:1-2","James 3:13-18","James 3:2"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_james_003_6f81a31374fb8393b2540b6e"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"The Character of Wisdom","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"James does not present wisdom as forceful certainty. Its profile is peaceable, gentle, open to reason, merciful, impartial, and sincere. That description helps explain why coercive certainty conflicts with the kind of wisdom the passage commends.","verse_refs":["James 3:13-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_james_003_668afb95e22256038601d0b0"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"James in Context","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"The wider writing combines a letter’s greeting with sustained moral exhortation, wisdom contrasts, examples, questions, and prophetic warning. Labels such as wisdom writing, diatribe, sermon, or anthology can highlight real features, but none fully captures the whole work; its place of composition is not named.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_james_003_54f433e7d9dcb775b1753b19","reader_path_james_003_21d9f20f9363b3f5f59539ea"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}